const e=[{id:"sv-2-201",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A digital-services team has collected **300 free-text customer comments** from app-store reviews. There is no agreed problem statement yet. The Green Belt wants the team to sort the comments into a handful of **natural themes** so the team can see what customers talk about most. Which tool fits this need BEST?",options:["An affinity diagram","A fishbone (cause-and-effect) diagram","A SIPOC diagram","An FMEA"],answer:0,hint:"Group unstructured ideas or comments into natural themes → affinity diagram. List causes of ONE agreed effect → fishbone.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'A fishbone starts from **one agreed effect** and asks "what could cause this?". An affinity diagram starts from **many unsorted ideas** and groups them by similarity.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"There is **no problem statement yet** and the team wants **themes** from 300 comments. That is grouping, not cause analysis, so the affinity diagram fits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Once a theme becomes a clear problem (for example "app log-in fails"), the team can put it at the head of a fishbone. SIPOC and FMEA answer different questions.'}],final:"Group raw comments into themes → affinity diagram → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A fishbone needs one agreed effect at its head and then lists possible causes of it. Here there is no effect yet; the job is to group raw comments into themes.","A SIPOC frames a process at a high level (suppliers to customers); it does not group customer comments.","An FMEA scores how process steps can fail (S, O, D); it is not a tool for grouping unstructured comments."],trap:"A fishbone needs an effect at the head; no effect yet means it is too early for a fishbone.",bookRef:"Book p. 118; Syllabus 2.1.1"},{id:"sv-2-202",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:'Which Measure tool takes the causes listed on a fishbone diagram and places them in its **"Potential causes of failure"** column, next to a **Class** column (C, P or N)?',options:["The SIPOC","The value stream map","The FMEA","The Pareto chart"],answer:2,hint:"Fishbone causes feed the FMEA (Potential causes + Class C/P/N columns) and the X-Y matrix.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The FMEA template has a **Class** column (C/P/N) and a **Potential causes of failure** column. The book names the fishbone as the main source for both."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stem describes exactly those two columns, so the tool is the **FMEA**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"SIPOC, VSM and Pareto do not hold a list of causes with a C/P/N class."}],final:"Causes + Class columns → FMEA → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A SIPOC lists suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs and customers; it has no causes-of-failure or Class column.","A value stream map records cycle times, queues and yields per step, not fishbone causes.","","A Pareto chart ranks counted categories; it has no Class or causes-of-failure column."],bookRef:"Book pp. 119, 127–131"},{id:"sv-2-203",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:'In a fishbone session on *"SIM activations fail on the first attempt"*, the team writes the cause **"Agents must follow the 12-step activation checklist in the SOP."** How is this input normally classified?',options:["Controllable (C)","Noise (N)","Critical X (CX)","Procedural (P)"],answer:3,hint:"C = adjustable setting while running; P = standardised set of activities (SOP, form, checklist); N = cannot or will not control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines **procedural** inputs as those set by a **standardised set of activities** that complete a step, for example a fixed data-entry form or the recipe SOP."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 12-step checklist in the SOP is exactly a standardised set of activities → **P**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'It is not a running setting (C) and it is not outside our control (N). "Critical X" is a later label, not a fishbone class.'}],final:"SOP checklist = procedural → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Controllable inputs are settings that can be adjusted while the process runs (speed, temperature, timeout value). A checklist is a standard set of activities, so it is procedural.","Noise is something we think we cannot control, do not know about, or that is too costly to control. A written SOP is fully under our control as a procedure.",'"Critical X" is not one of the three classes on a fishbone; an input becomes critical only after data show it drives Y.',""],bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114"},{id:"sv-2-204",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A fishbone diagram is also known by which of these names?",options:["Affinity diagram","Tree diagram","Ishikawa diagram","Interrelationship digraph"],answer:2,hint:"Fishbone = cause-and-effect diagram = Ishikawa diagram.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The fishbone was popularised by Kaoru Ishikawa, so it is also called the **Ishikawa** or **cause-and-effect** diagram."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Affinity, tree and interrelationship diagrams are other management-and-planning tools with different shapes and purposes."}],final:"Fishbone = Ishikawa diagram → answer **C**",whyWrong:["An affinity diagram groups ideas into themes; it has no effect at a head and no cause bones.","A tree diagram breaks a goal (or a CTQ) into ever finer levels; it is not organised around one effect and its causes.","","An interrelationship digraph draws arrows between issues to find drivers; it is a different planning tool."],bookRef:"Book pp. 98, 118"},{id:"sv-2-205",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A network operations centre builds a fishbone for *"Rural cell sites go off-air for more than 30 minutes."* Using the 6M categories, which cause belongs on the **Mother Nature (Environment)** bone?',options:["An out-of-date fault-handling runbook","Worn power connectors in the site cabinet","Lightning strikes and storms near the towers","New NOC engineers not trained on remote resets"],answer:2,hint:"6Ms: Man, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature (environment: weather, temperature, humidity).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Mother Nature** bone holds environmental conditions such as weather, heat, humidity and dust."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Lightning and storms are weather → **Environment**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Runbook = Method; connectors = Machine or Material; training = People."}],final:"Weather = Mother Nature bone → answer **C**",whyWrong:["An out-of-date runbook is a **Method** cause: it is about how the work is done.","Worn connectors are a **Machine** or **Material** cause: they are part of the equipment.","","Untrained engineers are a **Man (People)** cause."],bookRef:"Book p. 118; Syllabus 2.1.1"},{id:"sv-2-206",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"On a fishbone diagram, an input should be classed as **noise (N)** only when it is physically impossible to control. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Noise = we think we cannot control it, OR we do not know about it, OR it is too expensive or difficult to control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines noise inputs as those we **think we cannot control**, **do not know about**, or that are **too expensive or difficult to control**."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:'Cost or difficulty is enough to class an input as noise. "Physically impossible" is far too narrow, so the statement is **False**.'}],final:'Noise also covers "too costly or difficult to control" → **False** → answer **B**',whyWrong:["The book gives three reasons for noise, not one: we think we cannot control it, we do not know about it, or it is too expensive or difficult to control.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114"},{id:"sv-2-207",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"A fishbone is normally built by the project team rather than by the Green Belt alone. All of the following are good reasons for this EXCEPT:",options:["It captures the knowledge of the people who do the work","It builds buy-in for the actions that follow","Walking through each process step with the team surfaces more possible causes","The team's fishbone gives statistical proof of which cause matters most"],answer:3,hint:'A fishbone is organised team brainstorming — "not real data". Proof comes later from data (Analyze).',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'A fishbone collects team knowledge and builds ownership. The book warns that such team tools are **"not real data"**.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Three options describe genuine team benefits. The odd one out claims **statistical proof**, which a fishbone can never give."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Proof that a cause matters comes from data analysis in Analyze, not from the brainstorm."}],final:"A fishbone gives no statistical proof → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is a real benefit: the people who run the process know its causes best.","This is a real benefit: people support solutions to causes they helped find.","This is a real benefit: using the process map step by step helps the team find more X's.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 98, 118"},{id:"sv-2-208",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:'A team writes **"Customer complaints"** at the head of its fishbone. After an hour the diagram has over 90 causes covering billing errors, slow repairs and rude staff, and the team cannot agree on anything. What should the Green Belt do?',options:["Add more bones so that every one of the 90 causes has its own category","Ask the team to vote now on the top three causes and start fixing them","Use a Pareto chart of complaint categories to pick one specific effect, then build a fishbone for that effect","Skip the fishbone and score all 90 causes directly in an FMEA"],answer:2,hint:"One fishbone = one specific, measurable effect. Too broad an effect → narrow it first (e.g. with a Pareto).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The head of a fishbone must be **one specific, measurable effect** from the problem statement. A vague head mixes several problems and makes the diagram unmanageable."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'"Customer complaints" covers billing, repairs and staff behaviour. A Pareto of complaint categories shows which one is biggest; that category becomes the effect of a focused fishbone.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Adding bones, voting or jumping to an FMEA all keep the vague effect."}],final:"Narrow the effect first, then build the fishbone → answer **C**",whyWrong:["More bones do not fix the real problem: the effect at the head is too broad, mixing several different problems.","Voting on causes of a vague, mixed effect would be guesswork; the effect must first be made specific.","","Moving 90 unfocused causes into an FMEA carries the same vague effect forward and wastes effort."],trap:"A messy fishbone usually means the effect at the head is too broad.",bookRef:"Book pp. 98, 118; Syllabus 2.1.1"},{id:"sv-2-209",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team enters its fishbone ideas into the Minitab worksheet used for **Quality Tools > Cause-and-Effect**. The effect is *"Fibre repair takes longer than the 8-hour SLA."*

\`\`\`
Cause-and-Effect Diagram — worksheet columns

Man                    Machine                 Methods
Technician new to GPON Splicer needs repair    No triage before dispatch
Shift handover gaps    Old OTDR firmware       Spares ordered after site visit

Material               Measurements            Environment
Wrong patch-lead stock Clock starts at ticket  Monsoon flooding of ducts
Handover checklist     Repair time rounded     Night-time road closures
  not updated            to the hour
\`\`\`

Which cause is entered under the **wrong** category?`,options:['"Old OTDR firmware" under Machine','"Repair time rounded to the hour" under Measurements','"Handover checklist not updated" under Material','"Monsoon flooding of ducts" under Environment'],answer:2,hint:"Methods = how the work is done (procedures, checklists, rules). Material = physical items consumed or used.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each 6M bone has a meaning: **Methods** = procedures and ways of working; **Material** = the physical items used, such as parts and cables."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"A handover **checklist** is a procedure, so it belongs under **Methods**, not Material. The other causes sit on sensible bones."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Firmware → Machine, rounding of recorded time → Measurements, flooding → Environment are all correct."}],final:"A checklist is a Method, not a Material → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Firmware on a test instrument is part of the equipment, so Machine is a correct home.","How repair time is recorded is a measurement issue, so Measurements is correct.","","Flooding is a weather condition, so Environment (Mother Nature) is correct."],bookRef:"Book pp. 98, 118; Syllabus 2.1.1"},{id:"sv-2-210",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`During a fishbone session on *"Average fault-restore time is 6.2 hours"*, an engineer says: "The **Measurement** bone is pointless — measurement can't make faults take longer to fix." The team notes that tickets are often closed hours after the fix, when someone remembers to update the system. What is the BEST response?`,options:["Keep the Measurement bone: the way restore time is recorded can create apparent variation in Y, so the measurement system must be checked","Remove the Measurement bone, because only causes that physically delay repairs belong on a fishbone","Move the late-closure issue to the head of the diagram, because it is the real effect","Ignore the issue, because measurement problems are handled only in the Control phase"],answer:0,hint:"Measurement is one of the 6Ms: a poor measurement system can create or hide variation in Y. MSA is a Measure activity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Y we analyse is the **measured** Y. If the measurement adds error (late closure, rounding), the data show variation that is not in the real process."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Tickets closed hours after the fix inflate the recorded restore time. That is a measurement cause, so it stays on the **Measurement** bone and the team should plan an MSA."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Removing the bone, changing the effect or postponing to Control would let bad data drive the project."}],final:"Measurement error inflates the recorded Y → keep the bone and check the measurement system → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A fishbone lists every possible cause of the **observed** Y, and a recording error changes the observed Y even when the repair itself is fast.","Late closure is a possible cause of the high recorded time, not the effect itself; the effect stays as stated.","Measurement quality is checked in **Measure** (MSA comes before baseline analysis), not left until Control."],bookRef:"Book p. 118; Syllabus 2.1.1, 2.3"},{id:"sv-2-211",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:'A value stream map data box for the **"Validate porting request"** step shows a queue of **3,600 requests**. Each request takes a cycle time of **40 s**, and the step has **24,000 s** of available working time per day. How many **days of work** are waiting in the queue?',options:["0.15 days","1.67 days","6.00 days","40.0 days"],answer:2,hint:"Days in queue = (queue × cycle time) divided by available time per day.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Queue = 3,600 requests, cycle time = 40 s, available time = 24,000 s per day."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Days in queue}=\\dfrac{\\text{queue}\\times\\text{cycle time}}{\\text{available time per day}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Days in queue}=\\dfrac{3{,}600\\times 40}{24{,}000}=\\dfrac{144{,}000}{24{,}000}=6.00$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Six working days of work sit in front of this step. Comparing this figure across steps shows where the biggest queue (the bottleneck) is."}],final:"Days in queue = 6.00 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Divided the queue by the available time and forgot to multiply by the cycle time.","Divided by a full calendar day (86,400 s) instead of the 24,000 s of available working time.","","Converted the total work (144,000 s) into hours instead of dividing by the available time per day."],trap:"Use available working time, not 24 hours, and multiply by the cycle time.",bookRef:"Book pp. 105–106"},{id:"sv-2-212",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a standard process map, what does a **parallelogram** represent?",options:["A decision with Yes and No paths","Data","An activity that starts with a verb","The start or end of the process"],answer:1,hint:"Rectangle = activity; diamond = decision; parallelogram = data; ellipse = start/end; arrow = flow.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's standard symbols: rectangle = activity, **parallelogram = data**, diamond = decision, ellipse = start or end, arrow = direction of flow."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Diamond and parallelogram are the two shapes most often mixed up. The diamond asks a Yes/No question; the parallelogram shows data in or out."}],final:"Parallelogram = data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A decision is drawn as a **diamond**.","","An activity is drawn as a **rectangle**.","The start or end is drawn as an **ellipse** (oval)."],bookRef:"Book p. 101"},{id:"sv-2-213",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a **swim-lane (deployment)** map of a business-order process, where are bottlenecks and queues MOST likely to form?",options:["At the first activity in the top lane","At every decision diamond, whatever lane it is in","At the end ellipse, where the customer receives the output","Where the work crosses from one lane to another (hand-offs)"],answer:3,hint:"Each lane crossing is a supplier–customer hand-off; hand-offs are where queues and bottlenecks usually form.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every time work crosses a lane there is a **supplier–customer hand-off**. The book says this is **where bottlenecks and queues usually form**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So the team should look first at the lane crossings on its swim-lane map."}],final:"Queues form at lane crossings → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The first activity has no hand-off by default; position on the page says nothing about queues.","A decision can slow work, but the book points to lane crossings as the usual home of queues.","The end point is where the output leaves the process; it is not where internal queues build up.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 103"},{id:"sv-2-214",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Using the book's preferred **team approach** to building a process map, put these activities in order:

- **P** Tape one sheet on the wall for each macro step
- **Q** Agree the macro (Level 1) map with the team
- **R** Verify the map by walking the process
- **S** Each process member writes one task per post-it, with its actual work time, and the team consolidates them into a Level 2 map`,options:["P → Q → S → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:1,hint:"Macro map → one sheet per macro step → post-its (one task each, work time only) → consolidate to Level 2 → walk the process to verify.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The team approach builds the map top-down: agree the big picture first, then fill in the tasks under each macro step, then check against reality."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q (agree macro) → P (one sheet per macro step) → S (post-it tasks, consolidate) → R (walk the process)."}],final:"Q → P → S → R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The sheets on the wall represent the macro steps, so the macro map must be agreed first.","","The post-its are placed on the macro-step sheets, so the sheets must be on the wall before tasks are written.","Walking the process verifies a drawn map; you need the consolidated Level 2 map before you can verify it."],bookRef:"Book p. 107"},{id:"sv-2-215",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`A prepaid-SIM sale includes the step **"Scan and store the customer's ID as required by the telecom regulator."** The customer would not pay extra for it and it does not change the service, but the company cannot legally skip it. How should the team treat this step on its value stream map?`,options:["Mark it non-value-added but required (business-value-added): keep it and make it as small and fast as possible","Mark it value-added, because any step that is mandatory adds value","Remove it, because every non-value-added step must be eliminated","Leave it off the map, because regulatory steps are outside the project scope"],answer:0,hint:"VA test: does it change form, fit or function AND would the customer pay? Required-but-NVA steps are kept and minimised.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's two VA questions are: does the step change **form, fit or function**, and will the **customer pay** for it? If not, it is NVA. Some NVA steps (for example for **government regulations**) must still be kept."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"ID scanning fails both VA questions, but the regulator requires it. It is **NVA but required** (often called business-value-added): keep it and make it as lean as possible."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Mandatory ≠ value-added; NVA ≠ always remove; and nothing that really happens should be left off the map."}],final:"Required by law but adds no customer value → keep and minimise → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Being mandatory does not make a step value-added; it fails both VA questions (form/fit/function and willingness to pay).","Some NVA steps cannot be removed, for example those needed for government regulations; removing it would break the law.","The map must show the process as it really runs, including required steps that consume time."],trap:'VA vs BVA: "required" steps are business-value-added at best, never customer value-added.',bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-2-216",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"A SIPOC diagram helps a team answer all of the following questions EXCEPT:",options:["Who supplies the inputs to the process?","What do the customers require of the outputs?","Who are the true customers of the process?","At which tasks do decisions and rework loops occur inside each step?"],answer:3,hint:"SIPOC = high-level frame: Suppliers, Inputs, Process (4–8 steps), Outputs, Customers (+ requirements). Detailed decisions belong in Level 2–3 maps.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'A SIPOC is a **high-level** tool. Its "P" is only 4–8 macro steps, so it cannot show detailed decisions or rework loops.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Suppliers, customer requirements and customers are all on a SIPOC. Task-level decisions and rework need a detailed (Level 2 or 3) process map."}],final:"Detailed decisions and rework are not on a SIPOC → answer **D**",whyWrong:['Suppliers are the "S" of SIPOC, so this question is answered.',"Customer requirements are added to the outputs side of a SIPOC, so this question is answered.",'Customers are the "C" of SIPOC, so this question is answered.',""],bookRef:"Book pp. 111–112"},{id:"sv-2-217",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team exports the data boxes from its value stream map of a business-broadband order:

\`\`\`
Value Stream Map — step data
Step            Queue  C/T (s)  Available (s/day)  Days in queue   FPY
Order entry       600       30             25,200           0.71   0.97
Credit check    2,400       45             21,600           5.00   0.95
Provisioning      900       60             25,200           2.14   0.82
Activation        300       90             25,200           1.07   0.93
\`\`\`

Which statement is supported by the output?`,options:["Activation is the bottleneck because it has the longest cycle time","Credit check holds the most days of work in queue, while Provisioning has the lowest first-pass yield","Credit check is the quality bottleneck because it has the largest queue","Order entry should be fixed first because it has the highest first-pass yield"],answer:1,hint:"Speed bottleneck = most days of work in queue. Quality bottleneck = lowest first-pass yield (FPY).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A value stream map shows two kinds of bottleneck: **speed** (the step with the most days of work waiting) and **quality** (the step with the lowest yield)."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Days in queue: the largest is **Credit check = 5.00**. FPY: the lowest is **Provisioning = 0.82**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Longest C/T (Activation) is not the biggest queue; queue size is not a quality measure; high FPY is good, not a problem."}],final:"Credit check = biggest queue; Provisioning = lowest FPY → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A long cycle time per item does not make a bottleneck on its own; Activation has only 1.07 days of work waiting, far less than Credit check.","","Queue size shows a flow (speed) bottleneck; quality is shown by FPY, and the lowest FPY is Provisioning (0.82).","A high FPY means the step works well; it is a reason to look elsewhere, not to fix it first."],bookRef:"Book pp. 105–106"},{id:"sv-2-218",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'An enterprise customer states the requirement: *"The network must never fail."* While building the SIPOC, the team tests this requirement with **RUMBA**. What should the team do?',options:["Accept the requirement as written, because the customer is always right","Drop the requirement from the SIPOC, because it cannot be measured","Set the specification to the current availability, because that is what the process achieves","Negotiate with the customer to agree a measurable, achievable requirement, such as a monthly availability target"],answer:3,hint:"RUMBA = Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable. A requirement that fails any test is negotiated with the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book tests each requirement with **RUMBA**. If a requirement fails any letter, the team must **negotiate** with the customer."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'"Never fail" is not Achievable or Reasonable. The team should agree a clear, measurable target with the customer, for example a monthly availability percentage with an LSL.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Accepting it, deleting it or copying current performance all skip the negotiation the book requires."}],final:"Requirement fails RUMBA → negotiate → answer **D**",whyWrong:['"Never fail" is not achievable or reasonable, so accepting it as written fails RUMBA.',"Dropping a real customer need loses the voice of the customer; the book says to negotiate, not delete.","Specifications come from the customer's needs, not from what the process happens to deliver today.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-2-219",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A value stream map of a corporate-mobile order shows a total lead time of **12 days**, of which only **3.5 hours** is hands-on work. The operations manager proposes to **speed up the slowest agent** in the fulfilment team. Based on the map, where should the team focus FIRST?",options:["On the slowest agent, because the slowest worker always sets the lead time","On adding a final inspection step, so that errors are caught before activation","On the hands-on work time, because it is the only part of the lead time that can be measured","On the queues and waiting time between steps, because almost all of the lead time is waiting"],answer:3,hint:"Value stream map: lead time = work time + waiting. When waiting dominates, attack the queues and hand-offs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A value stream map shows the speed of the process and the size of the queues. It is very good for finding where time is lost."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only 3.5 hours of a 12-day lead time is work, so well over 95% of the time is **waiting**. The biggest gain comes from cutting queues and waits at hand-offs."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Speeding up one person or adding inspection hardly touches the waiting time."}],final:"Waiting dominates lead time → focus on queues → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The map shows that hands-on work is a tiny share of lead time; speeding up one agent changes very little.","Adding an inspection step adds non-value-added time and does not attack the waiting.","Waiting time is measured on the value stream map too; that is exactly what the map shows.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 103–106, 117"},{id:"sv-2-220",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The book's preferred way to describe a process is to give it a generic name, show its workflow with a process map, and describe its purpose with an operational description. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Describe a process = generic name + process map (workflow) + operational description (purpose).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the preferred way to describe a process is a **generic name**, a **process map** for the workflow and an **operational description** of its purpose."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"The statement lists the same three parts, so it is **True**."}],final:"Name + map + operational description → **True** → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book lists exactly these three parts: a generic name, a process map and an operational description."],bookRef:"Book p. 99"},{id:"sv-2-221",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`In a contact-centre X-Y matrix the input *"Call-back scheduling rule"* is rated **9** against the output **CSAT**. The sponsor raises the weight of CSAT from **5** to **8**. Nothing else changes. By how much does the input's total X-Y score increase?`,options:["3","27","45","72"],answer:1,hint:"X-Y score = sum of (rating × output weight). A change in one weight changes the score by rating × change in weight.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Rating against CSAT = 9; weight changes from 5 to 8; all other cells unchanged."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\Delta\\text{Score}=\\text{rating}\\times\\Delta\\text{weight}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\Delta\\text{Score}=9\\times(8-5)=9\\times 3=27$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The input's total rises by 27 points, so it may move up the ranking. The matrix is a living document and is re-sorted after such changes."}],final:"Score rises by 27 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used only the change in weight and forgot to multiply it by the rating of 9.","","This is the old CSAT contribution (9 × 5), not the change.","This is the new CSAT contribution (9 × 8), not the change."],bookRef:"Book pp. 120–121"},{id:"sv-2-222",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"When building an X-Y matrix, what scale does the book use to **weight each output (Y)**?",options:["0, 1, 3 or 9","1 to 10, where 10 is the most important","1 to 5, where 1 is the most important","Percentages that must add up to 100%"],answer:1,hint:"Output weights: 1–10 (10 = most important). Input-to-output ratings: 0, 1, 3, 9.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The X-Y matrix uses **two different scales**: outputs are **weighted 1 to 10**; each input is **rated 0, 1, 3 or 9** against each output."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The question asks about weighting the outputs, so the answer is **1 to 10**."}],final:"Y weights use 1–10 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0/1/3/9 is the scale for rating how strongly each **input** affects each output, not for weighting outputs.","","The book uses 1 to 10 with 10 as the most important; a reversed 1–5 scale would invert the ranking.","The weights do not need to add up to 100%; ties are allowed on the 1–10 scale."],trap:"Do not mix the two scales: weights 1–10, ratings 0/1/3/9.",bookRef:"Book pp. 120–121"},{id:"sv-2-223",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"To save meeting time, the project sponsor fills in the whole X-Y matrix **alone** overnight and sends it to the team as final. What is the main concern with this?",options:["The sponsor is not allowed to see the matrix until the Analyze phase","An X-Y matrix must always be filled in by the Master Black Belt","The matrix should capture the team's collective process knowledge; one person's view is a known roadblock that biases the ranking","An X-Y matrix filled in overnight cannot be weighted on a 1 to 10 scale"],answer:2,hint:'X-Y matrix = team-based prioritisation from collective ("tribal") knowledge; the Measure checklist asks who participated.',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines the X-Y matrix as **based on the team's collective opinions**. A matrix filled in by one person or by managers' best guesses is a listed **roadblock**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A single sponsor's scores reflect one view and lose the knowledge of the people who run the process. The ranking is likely to be biased."}],final:"One person's matrix loses team knowledge → answer **C**",whyWrong:["There is no rule hiding the matrix from the sponsor; the issue is who fills it in.","There is no such rule; the book asks for the cross-functional team, not a specific belt level.","","Timing has nothing to do with the weighting scale."],bookRef:"Book pp. 118, 229, 231"},{id:"sv-2-224",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`The X-Y matrix ranks *"Order-form free-text fields"* as the **top input**, with 24% of the total ranking. The process owner says: "Great — that's the root cause. Let's redesign the form this week." What should the Green Belt advise?`,options:["Agree, because the highest-ranked input in an X-Y matrix is proven to be the root cause","Drop the input, because any X with less than 50% of the total ranking is not important","Treat it as the first X to collect data on; the matrix is team opinion and the impact must be confirmed with data before acting","Re-run the matrix with a 1-to-10 rating scale to confirm the result"],answer:2,hint:`X-Y matrix = prioritisation from team opinion ("not real data"). Top X's → data collection → Analyze confirms or rejects.`,steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book warns that the X-Y matrix is **not real data**; X's the team believes are critical may prove unimportant."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The top-ranked input is the **first candidate to study with data**, not a proven root cause. Data collection and analysis come before a redesign."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"There is no 50% cut-off, and re-scoring with another scale is still opinion."}],final:"Top X = first to verify with data → answer **C**",whyWrong:['The X-Y matrix is "not real data"; a high rank shows where to look first, not proof of cause.',"There is no 50% rule; the matrix ranks inputs relative to each other.","","Changing the rating scale does not turn opinion into evidence, and the book prefers 0/1/3/9."],bookRef:"Book pp. 118, 122–123"},{id:"sv-2-225",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Halfway through Measure, the regulator starts publishing operators' **complaint-escalation rates**, and the sponsor asks the team to treat this as a new output. The team already has a completed X-Y matrix. What should the team do?",options:["Add the new output as a column, weight it, rate every input against it and re-sort the ranking","Start a completely new project, because an X-Y matrix cannot change once completed","Ignore the new output until the Control phase","Add the new output only to the FMEA, because the X-Y matrix covers controllable inputs only"],answer:0,hint:"X-Y matrix: created for every project, never completed, updated whenever a parameter changes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes the X-Y matrix as **never completed**: it is **updated whenever a parameter changes**, and new metrics often appear while weighting the Y's."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A new important output means a new **column**: weight it 1–10, rate each input 0/1/3/9 against it and re-sort."}],final:"Living matrix → add the column and re-rank → answer **A**",whyWrong:["",'The book says the matrix is "never completed" and is updated whenever a parameter changes; no new project is needed.',"Ignoring a new, customer-focused output would leave the ranking out of date during the very phase it is used.","The X-Y matrix lists all customer-focused outputs as columns; the new Y belongs there."],bookRef:"Book pp. 118, 120"},{id:"sv-2-226",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team exports its X-Y matrix results:

\`\`\`
X-Y Matrix — ranking summary
Input                          Ranking   Rank %   Cum %
X4 Call-script quality             186     31.0    31.0
X1 Agent tenure                    150     25.0    56.0
X6 CRM screen response time        132     22.0    78.0
X2 Staffing vs forecast             90     15.0    93.0
X5 Headset model                    30      5.0    98.0
X3 Office temperature               12      2.0   100.0
Total                              600
\`\`\`

The team's rule is to carry forward the **smallest set of inputs whose cumulative rank % is at least 60%**. Which inputs go forward to data collection?`,options:["X4, X1 and X6","X4 and X1","X4 only","X4, X1, X6 and X2"],answer:0,hint:"Read the Cum % column top-down and stop at the first row that meets the threshold.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An X-Y matrix result is sorted from highest to lowest ranking, like a Pareto. The cumulative % shows how much of the total ranking the top inputs cover."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Cum % is 31.0 after X4, 56.0 after X1 and **78.0 after X6**. X6 is the first row at or above 60%."},{title:"Decide",body:"Carry forward **X4, X1 and X6**."}],final:"Cum % first reaches 60% at X6 → X4, X1, X6 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","X4 and X1 reach only 56% cumulative, which is below the 60% rule; the team stopped one input too early.","X4 alone covers only 31% of the total ranking.",'X2 is not needed: the cumulative total is already 78% after X6, so adding X2 breaks the "smallest set" rule.'],bookRef:"Book pp. 121–123"},{id:"sv-2-227",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following statements describe the X-Y matrix EXCEPT:",options:["It is based on the team's collective opinions","It is created for every project","It is updated whenever a parameter changes","It statistically confirms which inputs are the vital few X's"],answer:3,hint:"X-Y matrix: team opinion, every project, never completed, a prioritisation tool — not statistical proof.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists four facts about the X-Y matrix: it collates potential X's against multiple Y's, it is **team opinion**, it is **created for every project**, and it is **never completed**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The odd one out claims **statistical confirmation**. The matrix prioritises; data in Analyze confirms."}],final:"No statistical proof from an X-Y matrix → answer **D**",whyWrong:[`True: the book says it is based on the team's collective opinions ("tribal knowledge").`,"True: the book says an X-Y matrix is created for every project.","True: the book says it is never completed and is updated whenever a parameter changes.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 118"},{id:"sv-2-228",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'An FMEA team is scoring **Occurrence** for the cause *"Wrong VLAN entered during circuit build."* Which source of information should it use FIRST to make this rating as objective as possible?',options:["The severity rating already given to the failure effect","Defect history and capability data for that cause","The list of current controls that might catch the error","The opinion of the most senior engineer only"],answer:1,hint:"Occurrence = how often the cause happens → defect history, capability studies. Detection ← controls/MSA. Severity ← effect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book links each FMEA column to a source: **Occurrence** comes from **capability studies and defect history**; Detection from controls and MSA; Severity from the effect."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"To rate how often a wrong VLAN is entered, use the **historical defect counts or capability data** for that step."}],final:"Occurrence ← defect history and capability data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Severity rates how bad the effect is; it says nothing about how often the cause happens.","","Current controls feed the **Detection** rating, not Occurrence.","One person's opinion is the least objective source; data come first, with team knowledge as a fallback."],bookRef:"Book pp. 131–132"},{id:"sv-2-229",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which of these actions on an order-entry failure mode is MOST likely to **lower the Occurrence** rating rather than the Detection rating?",options:["Replacing the free-text tariff field with a drop-down list so that an invalid tariff cannot be entered","Adding a supervisor audit of 10% of orders at the end of each day","Sending an automatic alert when a bill fails the tariff validation","Calling a sample of customers after their first bill to check for errors"],answer:0,hint:"Prevent the cause (mistake-proofing at the source) → lower Occurrence. Catch the failure after it happens → lower Detection.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Occurrence rates how often the **cause** happens. Actions that **prevent** the cause, such as mistake-proofing (poka-yoke) at the point of entry, lower Occurrence."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A drop-down list makes an invalid tariff impossible to enter, so the cause occurs less often."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Audits, alerts and call-backs all **find** errors that already happened: they improve Detection."}],final:"Mistake-proofing the input prevents the cause → Occurrence falls → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","An audit catches some errors after they happen; that improves Detection, not how often the cause occurs.","An alert after a failed validation finds the error once it exists; that is a Detection control.","Customer call-backs find errors late, after the bill; at best this helps Detection."],bookRef:"Book pp. 131–135"},{id:"sv-2-230",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Because Severity, Occurrence and Detection are each rated from 1 to 10, the Risk Priority Number (RPN) of a failure mode can range from 1 to 1,000. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"RPN = S × O × D, each 1–10.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"$$RPN=S\\times O\\times D$$"},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"Lowest: $1\\times 1\\times 1=1$. Highest: $10\\times 10\\times 10=1{,}000$. The statement is **True**."}],final:"RPN runs from 1 to 1,000 → **True** → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The minimum is 1 × 1 × 1 = 1 and the maximum is 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000, so the statement is correct."],bookRef:"Book pp. 123, 135"},{id:"sv-2-231",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put these milestones in the history of FMEA in chronological order:

- **P** The AIAG maintains the standard for design and process FMEAs
- **Q** FMEA is first used on the Apollo space missions
- **R** The car industry adopts FMEA
- **S** The US Navy issues MIL-STD-1629`,options:["S → Q → R → P","Q → R → S → P","Q → S → R → P","R → Q → S → P"],answer:2,hint:"FMEA history: Apollo (1960s) → MIL-STD-1629 (1974) → automotive (late 1970s) → AIAG standard today.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's history: first used in the **1960s on Apollo**; the **US Navy issued MIL-STD-1629 in 1974**; the **car industry** adopted it in the late 1970s; the **AIAG** now maintains the DFMEA/PFMEA standard."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q (Apollo) → S (MIL-STD-1629) → R (automotive) → P (AIAG)."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Apollo missions (1960s) came before the Navy standard (1974).","The car industry adopted FMEA in the late 1970s, after MIL-STD-1629 was issued in 1974.","","The car industry did not start FMEA; it adopted it after the Apollo and Navy uses."],bookRef:"Book p. 124"},{id:"sv-2-232",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team wants to analyse how its **order-to-cash billing process** can fail and let errors escape to customers. Which type of FMEA should it build?",options:["Process FMEA (PFMEA)","Design FMEA (DFMEA)","System FMEA","Equipment FMEA"],answer:0,hint:"PFMEA = failure modes in manufacturing AND transactional processes that may escape to the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says a **Process FMEA** covers failure modes in **manufacturing and transactional** processes that may escape to the customer."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Billing is a transactional process → **PFMEA**."}],final:"Transactional process → PFMEA → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A DFMEA analyses failure modes of a **product design** before release, not of a running process.","A System FMEA works at the early concept level, where modules tie together into a system.","An Equipment FMEA looks at the machines that make or test a product."],bookRef:"Book pp. 124–125"},{id:"sv-2-233",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A new home-router product already has a **Design FMEA**. The operations team now builds a **Process FMEA** for installing the router at customers' homes. According to the book, which rating MUST be carried from the DFMEA into the PFMEA?",options:["Occurrence","Detection","Severity","The RPN"],answer:2,hint:"DFMEA → PFMEA: the severity of a failure mode must be carried over.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book states that the **severity rating** of a failure mode in the DFMEA **must be carried into the PFMEA**: the effect on the customer is the same whatever the cause."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Occurrence and Detection describe the new process and its controls, so they are scored fresh, and the RPN is recomputed."}],final:"Carry severity from DFMEA to PFMEA → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Occurrence depends on how often causes happen in the installation process, which the DFMEA did not study.","Detection depends on the process controls during installation, which are new to the PFMEA.","","The RPN is recalculated in the PFMEA from its own S, O and D; only severity is carried over."],bookRef:"Book p. 124"},{id:"sv-2-234",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`An FMEA worksheet is exported after the team's actions:

\`\`\`
FMEA — action results
                                     Original           After action
Failure mode                         S  O  D  RPN       S  O  D  RPN
FM1 Wrong tariff applied             7  6  5  210       7  2  5   70
FM2 Port request lost in queue       8  4  7  224       8  4  3   96
FM3 Outage notice not sent           6  5  4  120       4  5  4   80
\`\`\`

Which statement is supported by the output?`,options:["The action on FM1 improved detection of wrong tariffs","The action on FM2 improved detection but did not change how often the cause occurs","FM1 still has the highest RPN after the actions","FM3's severity fell because its occurrence fell"],answer:1,hint:"Compare each rating before and after: the column that moved tells you what the action did.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each FMEA rating answers a different question; an action usually moves only one of them."},{title:"Read the output",body:"FM1: O 6 → 2 (prevention). FM2: **D 7 → 3, O unchanged at 4** (better detection). FM3: S 6 → 4 (severity changed, which is unusual and normally needs a design change)."},{title:"Decide",body:"Only the FM2 statement matches the numbers."}],final:"FM2: D fell, O unchanged → answer **B**",whyWrong:["For FM1 only O changed (6 → 2) while D stayed 5, so the action reduced occurrence, not detection.","","After the actions FM1 has the **lowest** RPN (70); FM2 (96) is now the highest.","FM3's occurrence stayed at 5; only severity changed (6 → 4). The two ratings are scored separately."],bookRef:"Book pp. 133–135"},{id:"sv-2-235",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Eight months after a project closed, the operator replaces its manual number-porting steps with a **new automated platform**. What should happen to the porting PFMEA?",options:["Nothing, because an FMEA is finished when the project closes","Delete it, because automated processes do not have failure modes","Review and re-score it, because an FMEA is a living document updated whenever the process changes","Keep the old ratings but add the new platform's name to the title"],answer:2,hint:"FMEA = living document: update when the design or process changes, when designs are reused, and after a problem-solving study.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book calls the FMEA a **living document**. It is updated whenever a design or process **changes**, when carry-over designs are used in new settings, and when a problem-solving study is completed."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A new automated platform is a major process change, so the PFMEA must be reviewed and re-scored."}],final:"Process changed → update the FMEA → answer **C**",whyWrong:['The book says the PFMEA is updated when a process changes; it is never "finished".',"Automated processes can still fail (wrong configuration, interface errors); the failure modes change, they do not vanish.","","Old ratings describe the old manual process; they must be re-scored for the new one."],bookRef:"Book p. 125"},{id:"sv-2-236",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement BEST describes the role of an FMEA according to the book?",options:["It eliminates every X that can cause a failure","It focuses on continuous problem prevention by controlling X's and prioritising risk, rather than on proving root causes","It proves statistically which X causes the most defects","It replaces the need for a control plan in the Control phase"],answer:1,hint:"FMEA = prevention and risk management: identify, rate and control X's; not elimination, not proof.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says FMEAs focus on **continuous problem prevention, not problem solving**, and that the FMEA **controls X's — it does not eliminate them**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Elimination, statistical proof and replacing the control plan are all beyond what an FMEA does."}],final:"Prevention and control of X's → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book says the FMEA **controls** X's; it does not eliminate them.","","An FMEA in Measure is built from tribal knowledge; statistical proof comes in Analyze.","The FMEA evaluates and feeds the control plan; it does not replace it."],bookRef:"Book pp. 123, 125–126"},{id:"sv-2-237",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`An FMEA team uses this extract of an occurrence scale:

| Capability of the cause | Occurrence rating |
|---|---|
| Cpk ≥ 1.67 | 1 |
| 1.33 ≤ Cpk < 1.67 | 3 |
| 1.00 ≤ Cpk < 1.33 | 5 |
| Cpk < 0.33 | 10 |

The cause *"Router delivered with the wrong firmware"* comes from a step with **Cpk = 1.45**. Which occurrence rating should the team enter?`,options:["1","5","10","3"],answer:3,hint:"Higher capability → the cause occurs less often → lower occurrence rating. Find the band that contains the Cpk.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Occurrence rates how often the cause happens. A more capable step (higher Cpk) produces fewer failures, so it gets a **lower** rating."},{title:"Read the table",body:"Cpk = 1.45 lies in the band 1.33 ≤ Cpk < 1.67 → rating **3**."}],final:"Cpk 1.45 → band 1.33–1.67 → rating 3 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A rating of 1 needs Cpk of at least 1.67; 1.45 has not reached that band.","5 is the band for Cpk from 1.00 up to 1.33; 1.45 is above that range.","10 means the cause is almost certain (Cpk below 0.33); a high Cpk gives a LOW occurrence rating.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 132"},{id:"sv-2-238",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"To audit retail-store activations, a Green Belt randomly selects **8 of the operator's 120 stores** and then checks **every activation** made in those 8 stores last week. Which sampling method is this?",options:["Stratified sampling","Systematic sampling","Cluster sampling","Simple random sampling"],answer:2,hint:"Cluster = randomly choose whole groups, then take every unit in them. Stratified = sample from every group.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Cluster sampling** randomly selects whole groups (clusters) and then measures **all** units in the chosen groups. **Stratified** sampling takes a sample from **each** group."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Eight stores are chosen at random and every activation in them is checked → **cluster** sampling."}],final:"Random whole groups, all units inside → cluster → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Stratified sampling would take a random sample from **every** store group (stratum), not all units from a few randomly chosen stores.","Systematic sampling picks every k-th unit from an ordered list.","","Simple random sampling would pick individual activations at random from all 120 stores."],bookRef:"Book p. 141; Syllabus 2.2.1"},{id:"sv-2-239",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A Green Belt draws a sample from the billing system's **list of all active postpaid accounts**. In basic statistics, what is this list called?",options:["The population","The parameter","The statistic","The frame"],answer:3,hint:"Population = all items of interest; frame = the identifiable list the sample is drawn from; sample = the subset measured.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines the **frame** as an identifiable list or subset that represents the population and is the source the sample is drawn from."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The list of active postpaid accounts is the source of the sample → **frame**."}],final:"The list sampled from = frame → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The population is all the items with the property of interest; the list is the identifiable source that represents it.","A parameter is a number that describes a population, such as μ or σ.","A statistic is a number computed from a sample, such as x̄ or s.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 141"},{id:"sv-2-240",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt's report uses these symbols:

| Quantity | Symbol used |
|---|---|
| Mean of **all** 12,000 tickets closed in Q3 | μ |
| Standard deviation of a **random sample of 150** of those tickets | σ |
| Mean of the same 150 sampled tickets | $\\bar{x}$ |
| Number of tickets in the sample | n |

Which row uses the **wrong** symbol?`,options:["The sample standard deviation row — it should be s","The Q3 population mean row — it should be $\\bar{x}$","The sample mean row — it should be μ","The sample size row — it should be N"],answer:0,hint:"Population parameters use Greek letters (μ, σ, N). Sample statistics use Latin letters ($\\bar{x}$, s, n).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Numbers that describe a **population** are parameters (μ, σ, σ², N). Numbers from a **sample** are statistics ($\\bar{x}$, s, s², n)."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"The standard deviation is computed from a **sample of 150**, so it is a statistic and should be written **s**, not σ."}],final:"Sample SD is s, not σ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","All 12,000 tickets are the whole population of interest, so μ is correct.","The 150 tickets are a sample, so $\\bar{x}$ is correct.","n is the sample size; N is the population size, so n is correct."],bookRef:"Book p. 141"},{id:"sv-2-241",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which of these measures is on a **ratio** scale?",options:["Net Promoter category (Detractor, Passive, Promoter)","Server-room temperature in °C","Customer region code (North, Central, South)","Monthly mobile data used by a customer, in GB"],answer:3,hint:"NOIR: Nominal (labels) → Ordinal (order) → Interval (equal steps, no true zero) → Ratio (true zero; ratios make sense).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'A **ratio** scale has equal steps **and a true zero** meaning "none", so "twice as much" makes sense.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"0 GB means no data used, and 20 GB is twice 10 GB → **ratio**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"NPS category = ordinal; °C = interval; region = nominal."}],final:"Data volume in GB has a true zero → ratio → answer **D**",whyWrong:["NPS categories have an order but no meaningful distances, so they are ordinal.",'Celsius has equal steps but 0 °C is not "no heat", so it is interval, not ratio.',"Region codes are labels with no order, so they are nominal.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 144–146"},{id:"sv-2-242",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are measured on a **nominal** scale EXCEPT:",options:["A store's ranking in the regional sales league (1st, 2nd, 3rd …)","The handset brand a customer uses","The name of the customer's tariff plan","The governorate where the customer lives"],answer:0,hint:"Nominal = names or labels only (same or different). Ordinal = ordered categories with unequal or unknown gaps.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Nominal data are **labels** with no order. Ordinal data have a **meaningful order**, but the gaps between ranks are not equal or known."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A league position (1st, 2nd, 3rd) has an order → **ordinal**. The other three are pure labels."}],final:"Ranking has order → ordinal, not nominal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Handset brand is a label with no natural order → nominal.","Tariff-plan names are labels → nominal.","Governorate is a label with no natural order → nominal."],bookRef:"Book pp. 144–145"},{id:"sv-2-243",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Attribute (pass/fail) data are preferred over continuous data because they need smaller samples to detect the same change in a process. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Continuous data are always more desirable: more information per unit, smaller samples, more powerful tools.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says **continuous data are always more desirable**: they show how far each unit is from target and need **smaller samples**."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"The statement reverses this. Pass/fail data throw information away and need **larger** samples, so it is **False**."}],final:"Continuous data need smaller samples → **False** → answer **B**",whyWrong:["It is the other way round: continuous data carry more information and need smaller samples.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 146"},{id:"sv-2-244",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:`To measure satisfaction with the operator's retail service, a team surveys only customers who visit the **flagship store in the capital** on weekday mornings. The results will be reported as "customer satisfaction across all stores". What is the main problem?`,options:["The sample is too large to analyse with descriptive statistics","Satisfaction is ordinal data, so it cannot be sampled","The sample was drawn from a frame that does not represent the population, so the results are likely biased","Weekday mornings give a systematic sample, which is always invalid"],answer:2,hint:"A sample only supports an inference about the population its frame represents. Convenience samples are biased.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An inference is valid only if the sample represents the population. The book stresses choosing a **frame** that represents the population well."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'Morning visitors to one flagship store differ from customers of all stores and all times. The conclusion "across all stores" is likely **biased**.'}],final:"Unrepresentative frame → biased inference → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Size is not the issue; a biased sample stays biased however large it is.","Ordinal data can be sampled and summarised; the scale is not the problem here.","","Choosing one time slot is not systematic sampling (every k-th unit), and systematic sampling is not always invalid."],bookRef:"Book p. 141"},{id:"sv-2-245",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab worksheet information for a fault-restore project:

\`\`\`
Information on the Worksheet
Column  Count  Name         Type  Values seen
C1        500  TicketID     T     TK-00001 … TK-00500
C2        500  Region       T     North, Central, South
C3        500  RestoreMin   N     12.4 … 486.0
C4        500  Reopened     T     Yes, No
C5        500  CSAT         N     1, 2, 3, 4, 5  (1 = very poor … 5 = excellent)
\`\`\`

Which column is stored as **numeric** but is really measured on an **ordinal** scale?`,options:["C3 RestoreMin","C2 Region","C5 CSAT","C4 Reopened"],answer:2,hint:"Numeric storage ≠ continuous data. Ordered ratings (1–5) are ordinal: the gaps between levels are not known to be equal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Minitab stores numbers as type **N**, but the **scale** depends on what the numbers mean. Ratings such as 1–5 have an order, but the gaps are not equal → **ordinal**."},{title:"Read the output",body:"C5 CSAT is type N with values 1–5 labelled very poor … excellent → ordinal. C3 is a true measurement (ratio); C2 and C4 are text."}],final:"CSAT 1–5 is ordinal although stored as numbers → answer **C**",whyWrong:["RestoreMin is continuous time with a true zero → ratio data, not ordinal.","Region is stored as text and is nominal (labels with no order).","","Reopened is stored as text and is binary attribute data (Yes/No)."],trap:"A column of numbers is not automatically continuous data.",bookRef:"Book pp. 142–146"},{id:"sv-2-246",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:"Order the four scales of measurement from the **least** information to the **most** information.",options:["Ordinal → Nominal → Interval → Ratio","Nominal → Ordinal → Ratio → Interval","Nominal → Interval → Ordinal → Ratio","Nominal → Ordinal → Interval → Ratio"],answer:3,hint:"NOIR: Nominal → Ordinal → Interval → Ratio.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each scale adds something: **Nominal** (names) → **Ordinal** (+ order) → **Interval** (+ equal steps) → **Ratio** (+ true zero)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"From least to most information: Nominal → Ordinal → Interval → Ratio (NOIR)."}],final:"NOIR → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Nominal (labels only) carries less information than ordinal (labels plus order).","Ratio carries the most information because it adds a true zero to the interval scale.","Interval needs order plus equal steps, so it carries more information than ordinal, not less.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 144–146"},{id:"sv-2-247",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Checking all **2 million** call-detail records of last month by hand is impossible. The Green Belt proposes a properly drawn random sample of **400** records. What is the main justification for sampling?",options:["A sample is always more accurate than measuring the whole population","Samples remove the need to define the population","A well-drawn sample gives a sufficiently accurate inference about the population for much less time, money and effort","Sample statistics are parameters, so no inference is needed"],answer:2,hint:"Why sample? Sufficiently accurate inference for much less time, money and resources — if the sample is drawn well.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says we sample to get a **sufficiently accurate inference** for **much less time, money and resources**. If sampling is done well, the sample represents the population."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"400 random records can describe 2 million records well enough at a tiny fraction of the effort."}],final:"Accurate enough, far cheaper → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A sample is not more accurate than a full census; it is accurate **enough** at far lower cost.","You must still define the population carefully; the sample represents it.","","Sample statistics estimate parameters; that estimate is exactly the inference."],bookRef:"Book p. 141"},{id:"sv-2-248",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Ten fibre-repair visits took these times (hours), already sorted: **4, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 46**. What is the **10% trimmed mean** (remove the smallest 10% and the largest 10% of values, then average the rest)?",options:["8.22 h","8.50 h","8.75 h","12.00 h"],answer:2,hint:"Trimmed mean: drop the stated % of values from EACH end, then average what remains.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 10, so 10% = 1 value from each end. Remove 4 and 46."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\bar{x}_{trim}=\\dfrac{\\sum \\text{remaining values}}{\\text{number remaining}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\bar{x}_{trim}=\\dfrac{6+7+7+8+9+10+11+12}{8}=\\dfrac{70}{8}=8.75$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The trimmed mean (8.75 h) sits close to the median (8.5 h), while the plain mean (12.0 h) is pulled up by one extreme visit."}],final:"Trimmed mean = 8.75 h → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Removed only the largest value (46) and averaged the other nine; a trimmed mean removes values from both ends.","This is the median (the average of 8 and 9), not the trimmed mean.","","This is the ordinary mean of all ten values, still pulled up by the 46-hour visit."],bookRef:"Book p. 149"},{id:"sv-2-249",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A Minitab summary of technician travel times shows a **variance of 2.25 h²**. What is the standard deviation?",options:["1.125 h","1.50 h","2.25 h","5.06 h"],answer:1,hint:"Standard deviation = square root of the variance.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Variance s² = 2.25 h²."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$s=\\sqrt{s^2}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$s=\\sqrt{2.25}=1.50$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The SD is back in the original units (hours), so it can be compared with the data and the specification."}],final:"s = 1.50 h → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Halved the variance instead of taking its square root.","","Reported the variance itself; the variance is in squared units (h²).","Squared the variance instead of taking its square root."],bookRef:"Book pp. 150–151"},{id:"sv-2-250",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab output for fault-fix times (hours) in two regions:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: FixTime

Variable  Region   N   Mean  StDev     Q1  Median     Q3
FixTime   North   60  14.20   5.10  10.60   13.80  17.40
          South   60  14.90   8.70   8.20   12.50  21.90
\`\`\`

Which statement is supported by the output?`,options:["The middle 50% of South's fix times spans about twice the width of North's (IQR about 13.7 h against 6.8 h)","North is more variable, because its median is higher","The two regions have similar spread, because their means differ by less than 1 hour","South's interquartile range is 21.90 h"],answer:0,hint:"IQR = Q3 − Q1 = the spread of the middle 50%. Location (mean, median) and spread are different questions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **interquartile range** is Q3 − Q1, the width of the middle 50% of the data."},{title:"Read the output",body:"North: 17.40 − 10.60 = **6.80 h**. South: 21.90 − 8.20 = **13.70 h**, about twice as wide. South's StDev (8.70) agrees."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Medians and means describe the centre, not the spread; Q3 alone is not the IQR."}],final:"IQR South 13.7 h vs North 6.8 h → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The median measures location, not spread; spread is shown by IQR and StDev, and both are larger for South.","Similar means say nothing about spread; South's StDev and IQR are far larger.","21.90 is South's Q3; the IQR is Q3 − Q1 = 21.90 − 8.20 = 13.70 h."],bookRef:"Book pp. 147–150"},{id:"sv-2-251",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`Minitab output for router set-up times:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: SetupMin

Variable    N  N*   Mean  SE Mean  StDev  Minimum     Q1  Median     Q3  Maximum
SetupMin  188  12  23.47    0.460   6.31    10.20  19.05   22.80  27.40    44.10
\`\`\`

How many rows does the column contain, and how many values were used to calculate the mean?`,options:["200 rows; 188 values used","188 rows; 176 values used","200 rows; 200 values used","212 rows; 188 values used"],answer:0,hint:"Minitab: N = number of non-missing values used; N* = number of missing values. Total rows = N + N*.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Minitab output **N** counts the non-missing values used in the statistics, and **N*** counts the **missing** values."},{title:"Read the output",body:"N = 188 values were used; N* = 12 were missing; total rows = 188 + 12 = **200**."}],final:"200 rows, 188 used → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","N already excludes the missing values; subtracting N* again double-counts them.","N* = 12 values are missing, so they cannot have been used in the mean.","The total is N + N* = 188 + 12 = 200, not 212."],bookRef:"Book p. 147"},{id:"sv-2-252",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab output for 100 chat-handling times:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: ChatMin

Variable    N   Mean  SE Mean  StDev
ChatMin   100   8.62     0.41   4.10
\`\`\`

The team leader writes in a report: *"A typical chat differs from the average by only about 0.4 minutes."* Which response is correct?`,options:["The statement is correct, because SE Mean measures the typical distance of each chat from the mean","The statement is wrong: individual chats typically differ from the mean by about 4.1 min (StDev); 0.41 min is the standard error, the precision of the estimated mean","The statement is wrong: chats typically differ from the mean by 8.62 min","The statement is correct, because SE Mean and StDev always describe the same thing"],answer:1,hint:"StDev = spread of individual values. SE Mean = StDev divided by √n = uncertainty in the sample mean.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **standard deviation** describes how far individual values typically fall from the mean. The **standard error of the mean** describes how precisely the mean is estimated: $$SE_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$"},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"$$SE_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{4.10}{\\sqrt{100}}=0.41$$ So 0.41 min is the precision of the mean. A **typical chat** varies by about **4.10 min**."},{title:"Decide",body:"The report confuses SE Mean with StDev, so the statement is wrong."}],final:"Typical spread = StDev 4.10 min; 0.41 = SE Mean → answer **B**",whyWrong:["SE Mean describes how precisely the sample mean estimates the process mean, not the spread of individual chats.","","8.62 min is the mean itself, not a measure of spread.","They are different: SE Mean = StDev divided by the square root of n, so it is 10 times smaller here."],trap:"SE Mean shrinks as n grows; StDev does not.",bookRef:"Book pp. 147–151"},{id:"sv-2-253",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab output for home-installation visit times:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: VisitMin

Variable    N   Mean  StDev  Median   Mode  N for Mode
VisitMin  120  61.80  24.60   58.50  35, 88          14
\`\`\`

What does this output MOST suggest, and what should the Green Belt do?`,options:["The mean and median are close, so the data must be normal and need no further look","Two modes far apart suggest two different processes mixed together; stratify the data (for example by visit type) and look at each group","The data have no mode, so the median should be reported instead","The large standard deviation proves the measurement system is not capable"],answer:1,hint:"Mode = most frequent value. Two modes (bimodal) often mean two processes, shifts or types mixed → stratify.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that a distribution with **two modes** is **bimodal**, which often means **two different processes are mixed together**."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Modes at **35 and 88 minutes**, each occurring 14 times, are far apart. Simple and complex visits (or two teams) may be pooled."},{title:"Decide",body:"Stratify by visit type, team or region and analyse each group separately."}],final:"Two modes → mixed processes → stratify → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Mean close to median does not rule out two peaks; a bimodal shape can be quite symmetric.","",'The output shows two modes (35 and 88), each appearing 14 times, not "no mode".',"A large spread can come from mixing two processes; it does not on its own say anything about the measurement system."],bookRef:"Book pp. 149, 158–159"},{id:"sv-2-254",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab output for average handle time (AHT):

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: AHT_min

Variable   N  Mean  StDev  Variance  CoefVar  Range
AHT_min   75  7.40   1.85      3.42    25.00   9.10
\`\`\`

Which value is expressed in **squared minutes**?`,options:["Variance (3.42)","StDev (1.85)","CoefVar (25.00)","Range (9.10)"],answer:0,hint:"Variance = average squared deviation → squared units. StDev = √variance → original units. CV = % (no units).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The variance averages **squared** deviations, so its units are squared (min²)."},{title:"Read the output",body:"$$s^2=1.85^2=3.42$$ Variance = 3.42 min², while StDev (1.85) and Range (9.10) are in minutes and CoefVar is a percentage."}],final:"Variance is in min² → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, so it is back in minutes.","The coefficient of variation is StDev divided by the mean × 100, so it is a unitless percentage.","The range is maximum minus minimum, so it is in minutes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 150–151"},{id:"sv-2-255",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt must summarise the baseline of **complaint-closure times**, which are strongly right-skewed with a few cases open for months. Which pair of statistics BEST describes the centre and the spread?",options:["Mean and standard deviation","Median and interquartile range","Mode and variance","Mean and range"],answer:1,hint:"Symmetric data → mean + SD. Skewed data or outliers → median + IQR (or range).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says to use **range or IQR when the data are skewed**, and the median is not affected by extreme values. The SD fits symmetric data."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Closure times are right-skewed with extreme values → report the **median** and the **IQR**."}],final:"Skewed → median + IQR → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Mean and SD suit symmetric data; both are pulled by the few very long cases.","","The mode can be unstable with continuous data, and the variance is in squared units and pulled by extremes.","Both the mean and the range are strongly affected by the extreme cases."],bookRef:"Book pp. 148–151"},{id:"sv-2-256",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team measures call-handling times on **one Tuesday** and finds a standard deviation of **1.2 min**. The same metric over the **whole quarter** has a standard deviation of **2.9 min**. What is the MOST likely explanation?",options:["The Tuesday SD is wrong, because a sample SD must always equal the long-term SD","A sample from one short period misses variation that builds up over time (shifts, drifts, day-to-day changes), so short-term SD is smaller","The quarterly SD should be divided by n − 1 again to match the Tuesday figure","The Tuesday data must be non-normal, which always lowers the standard deviation"],answer:1,hint:"Short-term samples capture only within-period variation; long-term data also contain shifts and drifts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book warns that a sample is usually taken over a **short period**, so it can miss variation that builds up **over time**. A one-day SD can be smaller than the true long-term SD."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"One Tuesday shows only that day's variation. Over a quarter, staffing changes, day-of-week effects and drifts add variation → 2.9 min."}],final:"Short-term sample misses long-term variation → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A sample SD can differ from the long-term SD; that is the book's point, not an error.","","Both SDs are already computed correctly; dividing again would be a calculation error.","Normality does not automatically lower the SD; the difference comes from the length of the period sampled."],trap:"This idea returns in capability as short-term (within) versus long-term (overall) sigma.",bookRef:"Book p. 151"},{id:"sv-2-257",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A team wants a single "typical" figure for monthly roaming charges. Most values are similar, but a handful of customers have extreme bills. The team wants a figure that still uses **most of the data values** yet is **not pulled by the few extreme bills**. Which statistic fits BEST?',options:["The arithmetic mean","The mode","The range","A trimmed mean"],answer:3,hint:"Trimmed mean: remove a set % of the smallest and largest values, then average the rest — a compromise between mean and median.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes the **trimmed mean** as a compromise between the mean and the median: drop a set percentage from each end, then average the rest. It is useful when extreme values are possible."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The team wants to use most values but ignore the extremes → **trimmed mean**."}],final:"Uses most values, resists extremes → trimmed mean → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The ordinary mean uses every value, so the extreme bills pull it up.","The mode uses only the most frequent value; it does not use most of the data.","The range is a measure of spread, not of centre, and it depends only on the two extremes.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 149"},{id:"sv-2-258",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of these is a measure of **variation** (dispersion) rather than a measure of location?",options:["Median","Mode","Trimmed mean","Interquartile range"],answer:3,hint:"Location: mean, median, mode, trimmed mean. Variation: range, IQR, standard deviation, variance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book splits descriptive statistics into **location** (mean, median, mode, trimmed mean) and **variation** (range, IQR, SD, variance)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the **IQR** measures spread."}],final:"IQR measures spread → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The median is the middle value — a measure of location.","The mode is the most frequent value — a measure of location.","The trimmed mean is an average — a measure of location.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 147"},{id:"sv-2-259",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Why does the **sample** standard deviation divide the sum of squared deviations by **n − 1** rather than by n?",options:["Because deviations are measured from the sample mean, which is computed from the same data; dividing by n would tend to underestimate the population variation","Because one data value is always an outlier and must be removed","Because n − 1 makes the standard deviation equal to the standard error","Because dividing by n would give the variance instead of the standard deviation"],answer:0,hint:"Sample SD uses n − 1 (degrees of freedom) because $\\bar{x}$ is estimated from the same data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Deviations around $\\bar{x}$ are, on average, a little smaller than deviations around the true μ, because $\\bar{x}$ is fitted to the same data. Dividing by **n − 1** (degrees of freedom) corrects this."},{title:"Formula",body:"$$s=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum (x_i-\\bar{x})^2}{n-1}}$$"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No outlier is dropped, and n − 1 has nothing to do with the standard error or with turning a variance into an SD."}],final:"n − 1 corrects underestimation from using the sample mean → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","No value is removed; all n values are used in the sum. Only the divisor changes.","The standard error is s divided by √n, a different quantity altogether.","Dividing by n or n − 1 both give a variance; the SD is the square root in either case."],bookRef:"Book pp. 150–151"},{id:"sv-2-260",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"A single extreme value (for example one 400-hour outage) is added to a data set of ticket times. All of the following statistics are fairly resistant to it EXCEPT:",options:["The range","The median","The interquartile range","A 10% trimmed mean"],answer:0,hint:"Range = max − min, so it depends entirely on the two most extreme values.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **range** uses only the largest and smallest values, so a single extreme value changes it completely."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Median, IQR and trimmed mean all ignore the extremes to some degree; the range does not."}],final:"Range is driven by the extreme → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The median depends on the middle values, so one extreme value hardly moves it.","The IQR depends on Q1 and Q3, so one extreme value hardly moves it.","A trimmed mean drops the most extreme values from each end before averaging."],bookRef:"Book pp. 148–150"},{id:"sv-2-261",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"Daily handle times for **10,000 calls** are normally distributed and the process is stable. Using the empirical rule, about how many calls would you expect to fall **outside μ ± 3σ**?",options:["13.5","27","300","500"],answer:1,hint:"Empirical rule: 68% within ±1σ, 95% within ±2σ, 99.7% within ±3σ.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"N = 10,000 calls; normal; region of interest is outside μ ± 3σ."},{title:"Pick the rule",body:"About 99.73% lie within ±3σ, so about 0.27% lie outside (both tails together)."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Expected outside}=10{,}000\\times 0.0027=27$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 27 calls per 10,000 lie beyond 3σ — rare, which is why points beyond 3σ on a control chart signal a special cause."}],final:"About 27 calls → answer **B**",whyWrong:['Counted only one tail (0.135%); "outside μ ± 3σ" includes both tails.',"","Treated the 0.3% outside ±3σ as 3%.","Used the ±2σ rule (5% outside) instead of ±3σ."],bookRef:"Book pp. 153–154"},{id:"sv-2-262",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Battery back-up runtime at cell sites is normally distributed with **μ = 6.0 h** and **σ = 0.8 h**. The minimum requirement is **4.0 h**. What is the Z-score of the minimum requirement?",options:["−3.13","−2.50","−0.40","5.00"],answer:1,hint:"Z = (x − μ) divided by σ.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"x = 4.0 h, μ = 6.0 h, σ = 0.8 h."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$Z=\\dfrac{4.0-6.0}{0.8}=\\dfrac{-2.0}{0.8}=-2.50$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The minimum lies 2.5 standard deviations **below** the mean; the area below Z = −2.50 (from a Z table) is the expected share of sites that fail the requirement."}],final:"Z = −2.50 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided by the variance (0.64) instead of the standard deviation.","","Inverted the formula (σ divided by the difference).","Forgot to subtract the mean (divided 4.0 by 0.8)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 152–153"},{id:"sv-2-263",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team tests activation times for normality before a capability study:

\`\`\`
Normality Test: Activation Time (min)
Anderson-Darling

Mean        31.84
StDev        4.27
N              40
AD          0.678
P-Value     0.071
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, which conclusion is worded correctly?`,options:["Accept H0: the data are proven to be normal","Fail to reject H0: there is not enough evidence that the data are non-normal, so normal-based tools may be used","Reject H0, because the p-value is below 0.10","Reject H0, because the AD statistic is larger than 0.5"],answer:1,hint:"Anderson-Darling: H0 = data are normal. p > α → fail to reject H0 (normal enough). p ≤ α → reject (not normal).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For the AD test, **H0: the data are normal**. Compare the p-value with α."},{title:"Read the output",body:'P-Value = 0.071 > 0.05 → **fail to reject H0**. The data are "normal enough" for tools that assume normality.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'"Accept" or "prove" H0 is wrong wording; α is 0.05, not 0.10; the AD value is not compared with 0.5.'}],final:"p = 0.071 > 0.05 → fail to reject H0 → answer **B**",whyWrong:['A test never proves H0; we "fail to reject" it. "Accept" and "proven" are the classic wording traps.',"","The stated α is 0.05, not 0.10; 0.071 > 0.05, so H0 is not rejected.","The decision uses the p-value against α, not a fixed AD cut-off."],trap:'Never "accept" or "prove" H0 — we fail to reject it.',bookRef:"Book pp. 155–156"},{id:"sv-2-264",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab graphical summary of ticket close times:

\`\`\`
Summary Report for Ticket Close Time (h)

Anderson-Darling Normality Test
  A-Squared    0.29
  P-Value     0.602
Mean         12.74     Minimum       4.90
StDev         3.21     1st Quartile 10.60
Variance     10.30     Median       12.70
Skewness      0.08     3rd Quartile 14.95
Kurtosis     -0.21     Maximum      20.80
N               64

95% Confidence Interval for Mean     11.94   13.54
95% Confidence Interval for Median   11.80   13.60
95% Confidence Interval for StDev     2.73    3.89
\`\`\`

Which interpretation of **"95% Confidence Interval for Mean 11.94 to 13.54"** is correct?`,options:["95% of individual tickets close between 11.94 and 13.54 h","We can be 95% confident that the true average close time of the process lies between 11.94 and 13.54 h","There is a 95% probability that the data are normally distributed","The sample mean of 12.74 h will fall in this interval in 95% of future months"],answer:1,hint:"A CI for the mean brackets the population mean (μ), not individual values. Individual values spread about ± 2 SD.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **confidence interval for the mean** is a range that, with 95% confidence, contains the **true process mean μ**. It is narrow because it reflects the precision of the mean, not the spread of individual tickets."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Individual tickets range from 4.90 to 20.80 h, much wider than 11.94–13.54 h. So the interval cannot describe 95% of tickets."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Shape is judged by the AD p-value, and the interval is not about future sample means."}],final:"CI for the mean brackets the true mean → answer **B**",whyWrong:["That describes where individual values fall (a prediction-type interval); with SD 3.21 h, individual tickets spread far wider than this.","","Normality is judged by the AD p-value (0.602); the CI says nothing about the shape.","The interval is a statement about the population mean, estimated from this sample, not about future sample means."],trap:"CI (for the mean) vs prediction interval (for individual values).",bookRef:"Book pp. 155–157"},{id:"sv-2-265",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Repair times from two field-service vendors are tested for normality, pooled and separately:

\`\`\`
Normality Test (Anderson-Darling)

Variable          N    Mean   StDev     AD   P-Value
Repair_All      120   52.60   12.56  2.214    <0.005
Repair_VendorA   60   41.30    5.20  0.338     0.492
Repair_VendorB   60   63.90    5.60  0.291     0.598
\`\`\`

What is the BEST conclusion?`,options:["The pooled data are non-normal because two different vendor processes are mixed; analyse each vendor separately","Transform the pooled data so that one capability study can be run for both vendors together","Vendor B is non-normal, because its mean is higher than Vendor A's","Both vendors are non-normal, because the pooled test has p < 0.005"],answer:0,hint:"Non-normal pooled data + normal subgroups → mixed processes (special cause). Stratify before anything else.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says that when data that should be normal are not, they often contain **special causes**, and a mix of processes is a common one. **Stratify** and look at each group."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Pooled: p < 0.005 (reject normality). Vendor A: p = 0.492; Vendor B: p = 0.598 (fail to reject). The means differ by about 22 minutes."},{title:"Decide",body:"The non-normality comes from **mixing two vendors**. Analyse them separately."}],final:"Mixed vendors → stratify → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A transformation would hide the real cause (two processes with very different means); stratification is the first step.","Each vendor's p-value is above 0.05, so neither is shown to be non-normal; a higher mean says nothing about shape.","The pooled result does not carry over to the separate groups; each vendor alone gives p > 0.05."],bookRef:"Book pp. 157–158"},{id:"sv-2-266",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`Minitab fits a normal distribution to link latency:

\`\`\`
Probability Plot of Latency (ms) — Normal

Mean    42.00   StDev   3.500   N   150
AD      0.384   P-Value 0.391

Table of Percentiles
Percent   Percentile
      5        36.24
     50        42.00
     95        47.76
\`\`\`

Engineers want the latency that **only 5% of readings** are expected to **exceed**, based on the fitted normal. Which value is it?`,options:["36.24 ms","42.00 ms","49.00 ms","47.76 ms"],answer:3,hint:'The p-th percentile is the value with p% of the data below it. "Only 5% exceed" = 95th percentile.',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A percentile is the value with that percentage of the distribution **below** it. If only 5% exceed a value, 95% lie below it → the **95th percentile**."},{title:"Read the output",body:"The table gives Percent 95 → **47.76 ms**. The AD p-value of 0.391 supports using the normal fit."}],final:"95th percentile = 47.76 ms → answer **D**",whyWrong:["36.24 ms is the 5th percentile: 5% of readings fall **below** it, not above.","42.00 ms is the median (50th percentile); half the readings exceed it.","49.00 ms is the mean + 2σ (42 + 2 × 3.5), which about 2.3% exceed, not 5%.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 152–156"},{id:"sv-2-267",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A normality test on loan-approval cycle times gives **p < 0.005**. The process owner says: "Non-normal data — the analysis is ruined." According to the book, what is the BEST response?',options:["Non-normal data are common in transactional processes and often point to special causes; go to the process and look for them","Delete the largest values until the p-value rises above 0.05","Stop the project, because Six Sigma tools work only with normal data","Collect a much smaller sample, because small samples are more likely to pass a normality test"],answer:0,hint:"Non-normal ≠ bad. Transactional data are often non-normal; look for special causes and stratify before anything else.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says non-normal data are **common in the transactional world**, are **not a bad thing**, and often contain **special causes** you can find by looking at the process."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The team should investigate (go to the process, stratify), not delete data or abandon the work."}],final:"Non-normal data are information, not failure → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Deleting inconvenient data hides the very information the team needs and invalidates the baseline.","Many Six Sigma tools handle non-normal data; non-normality is not a reason to stop.","Shrinking the sample only weakens the test; it does not change the process."],bookRef:"Book pp. 151, 154, 157–158"},{id:"sv-2-268",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team has four planned Measure activities. Which one relies MOST on the data being approximately **normal**?",options:["Building a Pareto chart of complaint categories","Drawing a SIPOC with the process owner","Using the mean, standard deviation and a Z table to predict the PPM beyond the SLA limit","Counting defects and opportunities to calculate DPMO"],answer:2,hint:"Z-table predictions of the area beyond a limit assume normality. Counting-based metrics do not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Many tools **assume normality** in their calculations, especially those that turn a mean and SD into an **area under the normal curve** (Z, PPM, capability)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Predicting PPM beyond a limit from a Z table is only valid if the data are close to normal. The other activities count or map."}],final:"Z-based PPM prediction assumes normality → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A Pareto chart counts categories; it makes no assumption about a distribution.","A SIPOC is a mapping tool; it uses no data distribution at all.","","DPMO comes from counting defects and opportunities; it does not use the normal curve."],bookRef:"Book pp. 153–154, 221"},{id:"sv-2-269",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What are the mean and standard deviation of the **standard normal** distribution?",options:["Mean = 0, standard deviation = 1","Mean = 1, standard deviation = 0","Mean = 0, standard deviation = 3","Mean = 50, standard deviation = 10"],answer:0,hint:"Standard normal: μ = 0, σ = 1. Any normal converts to it with Z = (x − μ) divided by σ.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says any normal distribution can be converted to the **standard normal**, with **μ = 0 and σ = 1**, using the Z-score."},{title:"Apply",body:"So a Z value is simply a distance from the mean measured in standard deviations."}],final:"μ = 0, σ = 1 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A standard deviation of 0 would mean no spread at all; the standard normal has SD 1.","SD 3 confuses the standard normal with the ±3σ span of the empirical rule.","Mean 50, SD 10 is a scaled score (such as a T-score), not the standard normal."],bookRef:"Book p. 152"},{id:"sv-2-270",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which two values fully define the shape and position of a particular normal distribution?",options:["The median and the range","The mean and the standard deviation","The mode and the interquartile range","The sample size and the skewness"],answer:1,hint:"A normal curve is set by two parameters: μ (centre) and σ (spread).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the normal curve is fully set by **two parameters: the mean and the standard deviation**. Each pair gives a unique curve."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Median, range, mode, IQR, n and skewness are not the defining parameters."}],final:"Mean and SD → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The median and range do not define a normal curve; two normal curves can share a range but differ in shape.","","Mode and IQR are summaries, not the parameters of the normal distribution.","Sample size is about the data collected, and a normal distribution always has skewness 0."],bookRef:"Book p. 152"},{id:"sv-2-271",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In a normal distribution, the mean, median and mode are equal. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Normal curve: symmetric, bell-shaped, mean = median = mode.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the normal curve is **smooth, symmetric and bell-shaped**, and the **mean, median and mode are equal**, at the centre."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"The statement matches the book, so it is **True**."}],final:"Mean = median = mode → **True** → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The normal curve is symmetric with a single peak, so mean, median and mode coincide at the centre."],bookRef:"Book p. 152"},{id:"sv-2-272",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, all of the following are characteristics of a process whose output is normally distributed EXCEPT:",options:["The data are skewed to the right with a long upper tail","Only random error is present","The process is free of assignable causes","The process is free of drifts and shifts"],answer:0,hint:"Normal output: only random error; no assignable causes; no drifts or shifts → symmetric bell shape.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists three characteristics: **only random error**, **free of assignable causes**, **free of drifts and shifts**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A long right tail describes a **skewed**, non-normal distribution — the intruder."}],final:"Right-skewed is not normal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This is listed by the book: only random error is present.","This is listed by the book: no assignable (special) causes.","This is listed by the book: no drifts or shifts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 151–152"},{id:"sv-2-273",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab box-plot statistics for handle time (min) by team:

\`\`\`
Boxplot of HandleTime by Team — statistics

Team    Q1   Median    Q3   Whisker low  Whisker high  Outliers (*)
A      5.2      6.8   8.9          2.4          13.9   —
B      4.1      5.0   6.0          2.0           8.4   11.2
C      6.0      7.4  11.6          3.1          19.5   —
\`\`\`

Which statement is correct?`,options:["Team C has an outlier, because 19.5 min is the largest value of all three teams","Team B has the highest median handle time","Team A has the widest middle 50%, because its whisker is longest","Team C has the widest middle 50% of handle times, and only Team B has a value flagged as an outlier"],answer:3,hint:"Box = Q1 to Q3 (IQR = middle 50%); outlier = beyond Q3 + 1.5 × IQR or below Q1 − 1.5 × IQR.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The box spans **Q1 to Q3**, so its width (IQR) is the spread of the middle 50%. Points beyond **1.5 × IQR** from the box are plotted as outliers (*)."},{title:"Read the output",body:"IQR: A = 3.7, B = 1.9, **C = 5.6** (widest). Only Team B lists an outlier (11.2; its fence is 6.0 + 1.5 × 1.9 = 8.85)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Largest value ≠ outlier; median of B is the lowest; whisker length ≠ IQR."}],final:"C widest IQR; only B has an outlier → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Being the largest value does not make a point an outlier; C's upper fence is 11.6 + 1.5 × 5.6 = 20.0, so 19.5 is inside.","Team B has the lowest median (5.0); Team C has the highest (7.4).","Whisker length is not the IQR; A's IQR is 3.7, smaller than C's 5.6.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 162–164"},{id:"sv-2-274",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A character dot plot of ticket response times is produced:

\`\`\`
Dotplot of Response Time (min)

      :
      :         :
      :         :         .
      :         :         :
   ---+---------+---------+---------+---
     10        15        20        25
\`\`\`

Every dot sits exactly on 10, 15 or 20 minutes. What does this pattern MOST likely reveal?`,options:["The process is bimodal, so the data come from two different processes","Granularity: response times are recorded in 5-minute steps, so the measurement resolution should be checked before analysis","The process is perfectly stable, because the values repeat","The data are normal, because the dots form a bell shape around 15 minutes"],answer:1,hint:"Dot plots show individual values and reveal granularity (values stuck on a few levels) that a histogram can hide.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that a **dot plot** reveals **granularity** — values sitting on only a few levels — which a histogram may hide."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Every value is 10, 15 or 20: the times are being recorded or rounded in **5-minute steps**. The measurement system has poor resolution."},{title:"Decide",body:"Check the measurement (resolution) before drawing conclusions about the process."}],final:"Values on a 5-minute grid → granularity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Three stacks on a regular 5-minute grid show rounding, not two separate peaks of a mixed process.","","Repeated values show the measurement steps, not stability; stability needs data in time order.","Normality cannot be judged from a few stacked values on a coarse grid; use a normality test on properly measured data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 161–162; Syllabus 2.3"},{id:"sv-2-275",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A churn analyst wants to see whether the **weekly churn rate has been drifting upward** over the last 52 weeks. Which graph should she use FIRST?",options:["A histogram","A box plot","A time series plot","A Pareto chart"],answer:2,hint:"Over time, trend, drift, shift → time series plot (run chart) or control chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **time series plot** shows data **in the order they were produced**, so it reveals trends, shifts and cycles. The book says every project should include one."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'"Drifting upward over 52 weeks" is a question about time order → time series plot.'}],final:"Drift over time → time series plot → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A histogram loses the time order, so it cannot show a drift.","A single box plot summarises all 52 values together and hides their order.","","A Pareto chart ranks categories by size; it has no time axis."],bookRef:"Book pp. 167–169"},{id:"sv-2-276",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Each new fibre activation is recorded as **Pass** or **Fail**, together with the **measured optical signal level (dBm)** at the customer's premises. The team wants a graph that shows whether signal level differs between passes and fails. Which graph does the book recommend?",options:["A scatter plot of signal level against Pass/Fail coded as 0 and 1","A time series plot of the pass rate","A histogram of Pass/Fail counts","A transposed box plot, with signal level as the continuous variable and Pass/Fail plotted on the y-axis"],answer:3,hint:"Attribute Y (pass/fail) with a continuous X → box plot of X by the Y groups, with pass/fail on the y-axis (transposed).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says that if the output is **pass/fail**, it must be plotted on the **y-axis**, and it uses a **transposed box plot** to compare the continuous X for each outcome."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Y = Pass/Fail (attribute), X = signal level (continuous) → transposed box plot."}],final:"Pass/fail Y with continuous X → transposed box plot → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A 0/1 scatter puts all points on two horizontal lines and hides the spread; the book uses a transposed box plot.","A time series of pass rate shows change over time, not how signal level relates to pass or fail.","A histogram of two categories just shows two counts and ignores signal level.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 165"},{id:"sv-2-277",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Box plots of resolution time for two support desks look almost identical: same median, same box and same whiskers. A team leader suspects that Desk 2 handles **two very different ticket types** (quick resets and long investigations). Which graph should the Green Belt use to check?",options:["A Pareto chart of ticket types","A wider box plot with notches","A pie chart of tickets by desk","An individual value plot of each desk"],answer:3,hint:"Box plots can hide a bimodal distribution; an individual value plot (or dot plot) shows every point.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book cautions that a **box plot can hide a bimodal distribution** — it may look symmetric. An **individual value plot** shows every point and reveals the two clusters."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The suspicion is two clusters inside Desk 2 → plot the individual values."}],final:"Check for hidden bimodality → individual value plot → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A Pareto shows how many tickets of each type there are, not how the resolution times are distributed within a desk.","Any box plot summarises with quartiles and can still hide two peaks.","A pie chart shows shares of a total; it shows nothing about the distribution of times.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 166"},{id:"sv-2-278",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:'The book describes graphing in the Measure phase as showing the results of **passive data collection**. What does "passive" mean here?',options:["The data are collected automatically by a system, never by people","Only data that the team did not ask for are used","The team changes one input at a time and watches the output","The team observes and records the process as it runs, without deliberately changing any inputs"],answer:3,hint:"Passive = observe the process as it is. Active = deliberately change X's (experiments).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Measure the team watches the process as it normally runs and graphs what it sees. This is **passive** data collection — no inputs are changed on purpose."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deliberately changing inputs is an **experiment** (active), which belongs to Improve."}],final:"Passive = observe without changing the process → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Passive refers to not changing the process, not to who or what records the data.","Passive data are still planned and requested; they are simply observational.","Changing inputs deliberately is active experimentation (DOE), used later in Improve.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 159"},{id:"sv-2-279",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put these steps for building a histogram in order:

- **P** Count how many values fall into each interval
- **Q** Divide the range of the data into equal-width intervals (cells)
- **R** Draw a bar for each interval with height equal to its frequency
- **S** Collect enough data values on the measure (for example 50 or more)`,options:["S → Q → P → R","Q → S → P → R","S → P → Q → R","S → Q → R → P"],answer:0,hint:"Histogram: data → equal intervals → frequency per interval → bars (height = frequency).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A histogram divides the horizontal axis into **equal intervals**; the height of each bar is the **frequency** of values in that interval."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Collect data (S) → define intervals (Q) → count per interval (P) → draw the bars (R)."}],final:"S → Q → P → R → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","You cannot set intervals across the range until you have the data and know its range.","Values can only be counted per interval after the intervals have been defined.","The bar heights are the counts, so counting must come before drawing."],bookRef:"Book pp. 160–161"},{id:"sv-2-280",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"A histogram of **500 call durations** from last month can show all of the following EXCEPT:",options:["Whether the distribution is skewed","Roughly where the centre of the data lies","Whether call durations trended upward during the month","How widely the durations spread"],answer:2,hint:"A histogram shows shape, centre and spread — but it loses the time order of the data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says a histogram shows **shape, centring and spread**. It groups values into intervals, so the **order in time is lost**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A trend over the month needs a time series plot, not a histogram."}],final:"A histogram cannot show a trend over time → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A histogram shows skewness through the shape of its bars.","The tallest region of a histogram shows where the data centre.","","The width of the histogram shows the spread."],bookRef:"Book pp. 160, 167"}];export{e as items};
